  "texts": [
    "How does a kettle boil water in just three minutes?",
    "Under its bottom hides a metal heating element.",
    "When you switch it on, electrons rush through it.",
    "They keep crashing into the metal's atoms.",
    "Each collision turns electrical energy into heat.",
    "A typical kettle uses about 2000 watts.",
    "The element heats the water at the bottom first.",
    "Warm water is less dense, so it rises.",
    "Cold water sinks, creating a convection loop.",
    "That rumbling is steam bubbles collapsing in cooler water.",
    "At 100 degrees, bubbles reach the surface and it goes quieter.",
    "One liter needs about 335 kilojoules to get there.",
    "At 2000 watts, that takes roughly three minutes.",
    "Then steam heats a bimetallic strip near the switch.",
    "Its two metals expand differently, so it bends and clicks off.",
    "Follow for more physics hiding in your kitchen!"
  ]
